# Testing without bespoke mocks

- **Prefer high-fidelity checks.** Exercise real code paths (real I/O where practical, real adapters, in-memory or container-backed dependencies) so tests show that behavior works—not that a one-off stub returns what the test hard-coded.
- **Avoid lots of ad hoc mocks.** Do not sprinkle per-test mocks that duplicate production contracts poorly; they often only prove the mock was configured to satisfy the test.
- **When isolation is required:** build a **small shared test harness** (fixtures, fakes, or test doubles) that **emulates production closely** and **reuse it across tests**—one place to maintain behavior, consistent assumptions, and less duplication.

Use narrow mocks only at true boundaries (e.g. external APIs you cannot call in CI) and keep them minimal and stable.
